  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nTraining of showers continue over Sullivan and Delaware Counties \nearly this morning as the main axis of rain shifts east over the \nHudson Valley. Based on the CAMs, there is potential that this rain \nwill last for a few hours. Even if the rain were to end here \nshortly, there are ongoing hydro issues that will take some time to \nimprove. For this reason, the Flood Watch will continue for Sullivan \nand Delaware Counties until conditions improve enough for us to end \nthe warnings. Elsewhere, some isolated showers will be possible this \nmorning. Clearing, light winds, and moisture from the rain led to \npatchy fog developing across the region. Fog will linger through the \nearly morning hours. With winds gradually picking up by daybreak, \nfog should not linger around much past sunrise.\n\nNortherly low-level flow will advect wraparound moisture into the \nregion later today, leading to shower coverage becoming more \nscattered by midday. There will be limited instability but likely \nenough to support isolated thunderstorms this afternoon. Scattered \nshowers will continue overnight and into Thursday as the low \npressure system slowly drifts east. PWATs will generally be 1.5 \ninches or less. Models do show slightly higher PWATs in northern \nareas of the CWA. Despite the potential for slow moving showers, \nrain will generally be light. Areas with the highest PoPs are also \nareas that received little rain yesterday. Given this, the Flood \nWatch for the other counties was cancelled. Conditions will dry out \nThursday night as a ridge of high pressure begins to build in.\n\nRain showers and cloudy skies will keep temperatures in the 70s \nduring the day. Overnight, temperatures will fall into the 50s and \n60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSurface high pressure will keep conditions mostly dry through the \nstart of the weekend. Weak ridge riders may try to kick off a \ncouple of showers late Friday, though moisture will be more limited. \nConditions will trend warmer this period with highs around and above \n80s across the region. Dewpoints will be in the mid 60s, so it may \nfeel a little muggy.\n\nThe second half of the weekend become a bit more uncertain. A weak \nsurface low pressure will develop over the lower Mississippi River \nValley Saturday and it drifts eastward. With high pressure over the \nAtlantic and this low near the Ohio River Valley, flow around these \nsystems will advect moisture northward leading to showers developing \nSunday. A frontal system then passes through late Sunday into Monday \nkeeping rain around early next week. Model guidance diverge on what \nhappens after the front moves through. Solutions like the ECMWF keep \nan upper level low present and the high pressure closer to the coast \nwith rain showers on Tuesday. Other solutions like the GFS have a \ntrough and dry northwest flow over the region. Given the \nuncertainty, chances for showers are forecasted Sunday through \nTuesday with the highest chances on Sunday.",
